            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Turns every true into false and every false into true, element by element.\n\nUse it to flip a mask: the cells NOT above a threshold, the frames where a hand is NOT moving, everything outside a selected region, the values that are NOT nan. With t.where it selects the other branch; with t.logical_and it subtracts one condition from another (A and not B: true in A but not in B).\n\nAny number other than 0 counts as true and 0 as false, so not of 2.5 is false and not of 0 is true.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.logical_not\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe tensor (a list is turned into one). Every input is flipped and sent.\n\noutput_type:\nAn option, under the node: bool (the default), int (1 and 0) or float (1.0 and 0.0). Choose int or float to plot the result, or to multiply with it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe opposite of each element, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: 1 0 0 1 becomes 0 1 1 0. Bottom: 0 2.5 -1 0 becomes 1 0 0 1 - only the zeros were false (output_type is set to int here, so they show as 1s and 0s). Edit a message and click it.\n\nRELATED:\nt.logical_and, t.logical_or and t.logical_xor combine two tensors. t.ne 0 makes a mask from numbers; t.where picks between two tensors by a mask. np.logical_not is the numpy version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 820,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Turns every true into false and every false into true, element by element.\n\nUse it to flip a mask: the cells NOT above a threshold, the frames where a hand is NOT moving, everything outside a selected region, the values that are NOT nan. With t.where it selects the other branch; with t.logical_and it subtracts one condition from another (A and not B: true in A but not in B).\n\nAny number other than 0 counts as true and 0 as false, so not of 2.5 is false and not of 0 is true.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.logical_not\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe tensor (a list is turned into one). Every input is flipped and sent.\n\noutput_type:\nAn option, under the node: bool (the default), int (1 and 0) or float (1.0 and 0.0). Choose int or float to plot the result, or to multiply with it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe opposite of each element, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: 1 0 0 1 becomes 0 1 1 0. Bottom: 0 2.5 -1 0 becomes 1 0 0 1 - only the zeros were false (output_type is set to int here, so they show as 1s and 0s). Edit a message and click it.\n\nRELATED:\nt.logical_and, t.logical_or and t.logical_xor combine two tensors. t.ne 0 makes a mask from numbers; t.where picks between two tensors by a mask. np.logical_not is the numpy version."
        },
